{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThree agents on 2026-08-05 reported contradictory answers to \"does ticking an AC checkbox\ninvalidate a plan-review attestation?\". The contradiction is a DIAGNOSTIC defect, not a\nbehavioral one, and the empirical answer is now settled (see Findings). Every attestation\nstaleness message today names a fixed, partly-fictional list of inputs, so a reader cannot\ntell which input actually changed and hunts for an edit that could never have been the cause.\nThe operator has approved fixing this broadly: wherever rebar reports an attestation error, it\nmust say WHAT CHANGED. That approval covers this ticket's whole scope, including the additive\nmanifest change described below.\n\n## Findings that fix the scope (verified in this checkout)\n\n- The material basis is a SINGLE composite sha256, built in\n  `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py` `material_fingerprint()` over exactly four keys —\n  `ticket_id`, `description`, `file_impact`, `children` — plus `file_impact_scope` when the\n  scope is `none`. Verified by reading `pass1.py:750-777`.\n- AC checkbox STATE is normalized out of that basis by\n  `pass1.py::_normalize_checkbox_state` (`pass1.py:741-747`, bug 330c). Ticking `- [ ]` to\n  `- [x]` therefore does NOT invalidate an attestation. Confirmed by running\n  `material_fingerprint` over a tick-only description pair: the hashes are equal.\n- \"AC\" is NOT a basis component. Acceptance criteria live inside `description`. Every current\n  message says \"description/AC/file_impact/children\", naming an input that does not exist.\n- A pre-330c attestation hashed the RAW description, so after a tick neither the normalized\n  recompute nor the `attest.py::_legacy_material_ok` grandfather (`attest.py:614-631`) can\n  match, and it reads `stale-material` with no component actually edited. This is the residual\n  cause the current message hides, and it reconciles observation 3 in the original report.\n- `children` is derived from OTHER tickets' `parent_id` at read time\n  (`relation_snapshot.py::live_material_children`), not from this ticket's own event log.\n- The signed manifest ALREADY carries per-child/prerequisite pins —\n  `plan-material-pin: <role> <id> <fingerprint>` parsed by `manifest.py::manifest_pins`\n  (`manifest.py:428-457`) — so the signed child-id set is recoverable from any pinned\n  attestation without any schema change.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd per-component fingerprints to the signed manifest and diff them component-wise on read.\n\nConcretely:\n\n1. New module `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/material_diff.py` holding two functions.\n   `material_components(ctx)` returns `{name: hash16}` for each basis key, hashing exactly the\n   same per-key values `material_fingerprint` already feeds into the composite (so the two can\n   never disagree). `explain_material_change(attestation, ticket_id, repo_root)` returns a\n   compact human string naming the components that differ.\n2. `manifest.py::build_manifest` emits one additive, deterministic line per component:\n   `material-part: <name> <hash16>`, sorted by name. `manifest.py` gains\n   `manifest_material_parts()` to parse them back. The lines are additive, so an existing\n   verifier ignores them and a pre-change manifest simply parses to `{}`. The prefix cannot\n   collide with `manifest_material()`, which matches `startswith(\"material:\")`.\n3. `attest.py::compute_validity` calls `explain_material_change` for BOTH `stale-material`\n   returns (plan-review at `attest.py:588-601` and completion-verifier at `attest.py:527-538`)\n   and puts the named components in `reason`. It also names the reopen timestamp on\n   `stale-reopened` and keeps the already-specific `stale-code` / `stale-head` wording (both\n   already name the drifted paths / the two SHAs).\n4. `attest_gate.py::claim_gate_check`'s `unsigned` message (`attest_gate.py:121-126`) states\n   both remedies, because rebar cannot distinguish \"never reviewed\" from \"review PASSed but\n   signing aborted\": run `rebar review-plan <id>`, or `rebar sign-review <id>` if a review\n   already passed. `claim_gate_check` already forwards `compute_validity`'s `reason` verbatim,\n   and `_commands/transition_close.py::_raise_plan_review_close_gate_error` and\n   `_commands/claim.py` render that same string, so fixing the source fixes the claim gate, the\n   close gate and `review-plan --status` together.\n5. The sites that re-word or drop the reason instead of forwarding it are pointed at the same\n   helper or made to carry the data they already hold:\n   - `generation.py:265-269` and `resign.py:198-208` — replace the fixed list with the named\n     components.\n   - `generation.py:270-273` — \"a dependency's plan material changed since the review\" names no\n     dependency, although the changed pin set is in hand; `resign.py:262-270` already names the\n     ids, so this one is brought to parity.\n   - `completion.py:178` — a child's closure-validity detail is currently\n     `f\"signature: {sig.get('verdict')}\"`, dropping the reason that explains it; it forwards the\n     reason too.\n   - `_cli/_llm_commands.py:118-141` — a `plan_review_sign_aborted` signing failure currently\n     renders the generic \"run `rebar sign-review`\" advice, which is wrong for the\n     `store-read-failure` reason (a re-sign hits the same unreadable read). It gets its own\n     branch naming the reason and the correct remedy.\n   - `attest_gate.py:166-167` — the docstring's verdict vocabulary omits `not-closed`,\n     `malformed-phase`, `incompatible-phase` and the `stale-pin-*` values; corrected so the\n     documented list matches what `compute_validity` can actually return.\n\n   These were found by an exhaustive sweep of every attestation refusal surface. Surfaces\n   deliberately left unchanged because they already forward the reason verbatim:\n   `_commands/gates.py::_claim_gate_reason`, `_commands/transition_close.py`, and\n   `llm/code_review/bugfix_size_gate.py`. `attest/opcert.py` and `signing.py` are also left\n   alone: their verdicts are cryptographic (`mismatch` / `foreign_key`), not staleness, and\n   already name the two keys or ids that differ.\n\nWhy this approach: the composite hash is one-way, so naming a changed component requires the\nold per-component values, and the only trustworthy source for them is the SIGNED manifest —\nanything reconstructed at read time could disagree with what the reviewer actually saw. The\nalternative of replaying the event store to `signed_at` is rejected: `children` comes from\nother tickets' state, so it would need a whole-store replay, and compaction squashes exactly\nthe old events where legacy attestations live. Adding the parts to the manifest is additive,\nauthenticated by the existing HMAC, and deterministic (no timestamps), so re-signing stays\nreproducible.\n\nAttestations signed before this change carry no `material-part:` lines. For them\n`explain_material_change` degrades in two useful steps rather than falling back to the old\nfixed list: it uses the signed `plan-material-pin:` child ids to report a `children` add/remove\ndiff when one exists, and when no component difference can be named it says so plainly and\npoints at the pre-330c normalization basis as the likely cause, with the remedy.\n\nNo gate outcome changes: every edit is inside the `reason` string of an already-failing branch,\nor is a new additive manifest line. No branch condition, threshold or verdict value is touched.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/material_diff.py` (new) — component hashing + the diff/explain\n  helper.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py` — emit and parse `material-part:` lines.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py` — component-named `stale-material` reasons (both\n  kinds); timestamp-named `stale-reopened`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest_gate.py` — actionable `unsigned` message.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py` — replace the fixed-list wording.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/resign.py` — replace the fixed-list wording.\n- `src/rebar/llm/completion.py` — forward the child-closure validity reason, not just the\n  verdict.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py` — a dedicated render branch for a `plan_review_sign_aborted`\n  signing failure that names the reason and the correct remedy.\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md` — state plainly that ticking a checkbox is attestation-safe, and\n  document the component-named staleness reasons.\n- `tests/unit/plan_review/test_94a3_material_diff.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n`pass1.py` is at 776 of the 800-line cap, so the new hashing helper goes in `material_diff.py`\nand `pass1.py` is not touched.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: an attestation whose `file_impact` changed reports a reason containing\n  `file_impact` and NOT `description` or `children`; likewise a description-only edit and a\n  children-only edit, one test each.\n- Checkbox pin (330c): an attestation over a description whose boxes are then ticked reports\n  `certified`, not `stale-material` — pinning the normalization that is currently only implicit.\n- Legacy/grandfather regression: a pre-330c attestation (raw-description basis) over an\n  unchanged ticket still reports `certified`, and one that cannot be grandfathered reports the\n  \"no component differs\" wording rather than a spurious component name.\n- Edge: an attestation with no `material-part:` lines and no pins still produces an actionable\n  reason and never raises.\n- Gate-outcome invariance: for each of the stale verdicts, assert `valid is False` and the\n  `verdict` value is unchanged from the current code — the same inputs are still refused.\n- The full existing suites `tests/unit/test_compute_validity.py`,\n  `tests/unit/test_legacy_material_grandfather_96d1.py`,\n  `tests/unit/test_attestation_invalidation.py` and `tests/unit/test_plan_material_pins.py`\n  pass unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `material_diff.material_components(ctx)` returns a per-component hash map whose keys are\n      exactly the `material_fingerprint` basis keys; verified by a test that asserts the key set\n      matches and that changing one component changes only that component's hash.\n- [ ] `build_manifest` emits sorted `material-part: <name> <hash16>` lines and\n      `manifest_material_parts()` round-trips them; verified by a test asserting the round-trip\n      and that a manifest without the lines parses to `{}`.\n- [ ] A `stale-material` reason from `compute_validity` NAMES the differing component(s) with a\n      compact magnitude (for example `file_impact: 13 -> 16 paths`) and does not contain the\n      string `description/AC/file_impact/children`; verified by one test per component, plus a\n      repo-wide grep asserting that literal no longer appears in `src/`.\n- [ ] Ticking checkboxes only leaves the verdict `certified`; verified by a test that signs over\n      an unticked description, ticks every box, and asserts `compute_validity` returns\n      `verdict == \"certified\"`.\n- [ ] A pre-330c (raw-description) attestation over an unchanged ticket still returns\n      `certified`, and an un-grandfatherable one returns a reason that says no component differs\n      rather than naming a component; verified by two tests.\n- [ ] `stale-reopened` names the reopen timestamp and `unsigned` names both `review-plan` and\n      `sign-review` remedies; verified by asserting those substrings in the reasons.\n- [ ] A `plan_review_sign_aborted` signing failure renders its reason (for example\n      `store-read-failure`) and does NOT advise `sign-review`; verified by a test that feeds a\n      verdict carrying that event to the renderer and asserts both facts.\n- [ ] A dependency-material change from `generation.py` names the changed dependency ticket\n      ids, matching `resign.py`'s existing wording; verified by asserting the ids appear in the\n      raised message.\n- [ ] No gate outcome changes: for every branch touched, a test asserts the same\n      `(valid, verdict)` pair as before, and `pytest tests/unit tests/integration` passes.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-gate.md` states plainly that ticking an AC checkbox is\n      attestation-safe; verified by a grep for that statement in the file.\n\n## Provenance\n\nThree independent agent reports on 2026-08-05, recorded in the original description above.\nRelated: bug 858c-786a-13be-4332 (the close gate cannot express an N/A criterion).\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785943732935739001,"uuid":"ab84e964-94e2-4509-9c6d-6426532aca2f"}